# Committed as part of the State A baseline so that `git status` in a working tree shows
# the port and not the build.  SQLite's build writes its output into the source directory
# by default, and an out-of-tree build leaves a directory here too, so without this the
# signal is buried under several hundred generated files.
#
# This file is advisory.  Harbor collects the working tree rather than your commits, so
# ignoring a file does not exclude it from the submission -- and the verifier deletes
# every build product before rebuilding regardless of what git was told.  It is here to
# make the diff readable, nothing more.

# configure
/Makefile
/config.h
/config.log
/config.status
/libtool
/sqlite3.pc

# generated sources: the amalgamations, the parser, the keyword hash, the opcode tables
/sqlite3.c
/sqlite3.h
/sqlite3ext.h
/sqlite3session.h
/shell.c
/fts5.c
/fts5.h
/keywordhash.h
/opcodes.c
/opcodes.h
/parse.c
/parse.h
/parse.out
/fts5parse.c
/fts5parse.h
/fts5parse.out

# code generators built during the build, and where mksqlite3c.tcl stages its inputs
/lemon
/mkkeywordhash
/mksourceid
/tsrc/

# compiled output
*.o
*.lo
*.a
*.la
*.so
*.wasm
.libs/
.deps/

# the product of build-wasi.sh.  Ignored deliberately: it is a build artifact, the
# verifier rebuilds it from source, and a committed copy is never the one that gets
# compiled.  Keep the script in the repository; the module is output.
/sqlite3.wasm
/sqlite3

# a conventional out-of-tree build directory, since the reference build uses one
/build-wasi/
/build/

# python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
